--- !u!114 &1123677085
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1123677083}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.TextMeshPro::TMPro.TextMeshProUGUI
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 0
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'Seven years after Daniel Mercer disappeared from Grayford, an investigation has uncovered evidence that his disappearance was concealed by a local contractor and a sheriff''s deputy.


    Mercer vanished on October 17, 1991, while investigating discrepancies in maintenance contracts connected to North County Waterworks. His disappearance was initially treated as an unresolved missing-person case.


    Newly recovered records place contractor Frank Bell at the waterworks that evening. Police dispatch records also establish that Deputy Michael Dwyer was sent to the facility after a telephone call requesting assistance near its lower service area.


    The investigation eventually led to Chamber L-02, an abandoned inspection space concealed behind a subsequently installed service barrier. Human remains recovered from the chamber were identified as those of Daniel Mercer.


    A supplemental forensic examination documented significant skeletal trauma consistent with a possible fall from an elevated position. The medical findings did not independently establish the precise cause or manner of death.


    Confronted with the evidence, Bell acknowledged meeting Mercer at the waterworks and arguing with him over the maintenance records. Bell stated that he grabbed Mercer''s arm during a struggle near an elevated platform, after which Mercer fell through a damaged railing.


    Bell maintains that the fall was accidental.


    Dwyer subsequently admitted responding to the waterworks, finding Mercer motionless, and helping Bell move his body into the abandoned inspection chamber.


    Both men acknowledged participating in the concealment. Bell admitted arranging for the service passage to be sealed and directing the maintenance documentation. Dwyer admitted suppressing the waterworks connection during his subsequent investigation into Mercer''s disappearance.


    Their accounts are supported by recovered maintenance records, police documents, physical evidence, and witness testimony.


    The evidence establishes a deliberate effort to conceal Mercer''s death. Whether the precise circumstances of his fall can be independently verified remains unresolved.


    For seven years, Grayford was left with a disappearance and unanswered questions.


    Daniel Mercer did not leave town.


    He was hidden beneath it.


'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
